\f4\fs26 Cracker double checked the address he had been given. This place seem for too plain for the residence of a wealthy night club owner, but the addresses matched. It was a average building in a district that housed mostly lower-level management on their way up the ladder or executives on their plummet down. The proprietor of the Stick it Inn was a businessman on a downward spiral with no hope of recovery and no parachute. \
\'93Mr Siddig?\'94 Cracker smiled and thrust out his hand. \'93Tony Hardy, but call me Cracker. Thank you for agreeing to see me. I understand you must be very busy at the moment.\'94\
\'93What do you want, Mr Hardy?\'94 Ahmed Siddig, the former owner of the \'93Stick it Inn\'94 had hit on hard times since the bombing. \'93If you are after money, you have come to the wrong place.\'94\
\'93I understand you have no plans to re-open your nightclub?\'94\
\'93That\'92s right, the fucken insurance company is refusing to pay. I funded the demolition myself, figured the insurance would come through eventually, but they are still wriggling and squirming. I now own the most expensive hole in the ground in the Primary Trade Dominion.\'94 Siddig would complain about the insurance industry to anyone within ear shot, whether they wanted to hear it or not. Being rendered destitute was very unnerving to the successful businessman and his misery perpetually sought release.\
\'93I may be of some assistance to you Mr Siddig.\'94 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi720\sl360\slmult1\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_Ps::0>
