\f4\fs26 The fire had been put out and the singed lab-coats were rushing about assessing the damage to the smoking laboratory. Spent fire extinguishers and tangled fire hoses were strewn about the floor as the extraction fans worked overtime trying to clear the room of smoke, allowing scorched lungs some relief.\
\'93Well, we have learned something today. Never spin the disc!\'94 The Johnny coughed out, trying to smile. Blue electric bursts had shot out of the disc like tiny lightening bolts, penetrating concrete, ballistic glass and metal lab equipment like they were tissue paper. Looking in awe at the scorched pattern on the wall, he could feel the disbelieving stares of his underlings, each wondering what sort of a fool had been placed in charge of this contract. \'93Okay, clean up. We will transfer everything to the other lab and make sure you keep those discs apart.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi720\sl360\slmult1\qc\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_Ps::0>
\f2 * * *
\f1 \
\pard\tx360\tx720\tx1080\tx1440\tx1800\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\pardeftab720\fi360\sl264\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f4\fs26 \'93What happened?\'94 Director Hardman yelled at the hologram.\
\'93We have been conducting some preliminary experiments in viral gene transfer. It is very interesting, Director. We know our predecessors used tardigrade DNA to horizontally transfer plant and algae genes and we have had remarkable success in replicating their processes. The genes, they, they transfer so easily, the possibilities are limitless. It is an astonishing field of research. Paths previously blocked are now open.\'94 Dr Kitsen was speaking way too fast for Damien to keep up. \
\'93You are babbling doctor, what happened?\'94\
\'93We tested new prototype pathogen. Our calculations assured us that no-one was in danger.\'94 The nervous junior geneticist stuttered. \'93It killed every red-head in the room.\'94\
\'93Anyone I know?\'94 \
\'93I would not think so, sir. They were just random lows.\'94\
\'93Document the experiment and safely store the pathogen.\'94 \
\'93Don\'92t you want us to destroy it?\'94 Dr Kitsen hoped so. His flame haired wife would not be happy to know he was behind a Ranga Holocaust.\
\'93No, it might have some commercial value in the future.\'94 Director Hardman did not want to lose an opportunity to create a valuable weapon. \
\'93But it only targets redheads.\'94 \
\'93Yes.\'94 The Director imagined a world without redheads and concluded they wouldn\'92t be missed. 
\f5\i Freckles and orange pubes? Alabaster skin that turns to cancer under the glare of a 50 watt bulb? 
\f4\i0 He visibly shuddered. \'93Continue with your experiments, I want you well practiced when the samples arrive. We will be on a tight schedule when the time comes, but in the meantime please conduct no further tests on live subjects.\'94\
\'93Yes, Director.\'94\
Director Hardman then contacted the drill team by audio link. \'93Agent Jones, I need an update.\'94 It had taken weeks just to build the giant airtight structure, sitting over the drill site. The outside of which was plastered with posters depicting a statue, some 5 metres tall, of a mother holding her dying child and a sign reading \'91The future site of the Great Viral Cleansing Memorial and Museum.\'92 The sign was covered with \'91The one-after-the-one is coming\'92 posters within hours.\
\'93We have plasma drilled as far as we dare, about 5 metres into the concrete. Lasers were not doing the job so we have resorted to concussion drills, but the concrete is very hard and we keep snapping drill bits. Our progress is slower now, but we have to be careful where we penetrate the labs. We must hasten slowly, if we poke a hole in the wrong spot there might be viral Armageddon.\'94 Agent Jones was under strict instructions from his handlers to go as slow as he dared without alerting the boss. He had been horrified to see how easily the plasma cutter and lasers sliced into the massive chunk a bio-crete and had acted quickly to stymie progress.\
\'93Hasten, Agent Jones.\'94 The clock was ticking.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi720\sl360\slmult1\qc\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_Ps::0>
\f2  * * *
\f1 \
\pard\tx360\tx720\tx1080\tx1440\tx1800\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\pardeftab720\fi360\sl264\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f4\fs26 The massive stage spanned clear across the enormous square hole that once was the Ulyssian Trade Tower, a historic and ugly stone relic, neglected for decades before its final demise. Elderly lime cement, exhausted by a century long vigil, had turned to grit and poured out of joints like hourglass sand, gradually bleeding away structural integrity. The small, but powerful terrorist bomb had found a perfect mark, cracking the structure to its foundations and rendering it nothing but smoldering rubble. \
Above the stage a huge banner read \'93Elvis Arts and Entertainment Benefit concert - Bring back the Stick it Inn\'94 (Description of stage equipment) A benefit concert was an event that no-one in the PTD had ever attended. The concept of earning money and then giving it away was so novel, that a massive crowd of Dawks and bemused citizens arrived on the street many hours before the music began. They began chanting, \'93Dawks, Dawks, Dawks!\'94 and singing their favourite Elvis tunes loudly and horribly. A few roadie-Elvises quickly tired of their songs being destroyed in such a manner, broke out their guitars and waded into the crowd. The crowd cheered loudly and parted to allow entry to the minstrels as they serenaded the throng, killing off the wretched singing that had offended their ears so. \
Underneath the frantic activity on stage, some 20 metres down on the floor of the vast excavation, was a menagerie earth moving of machines, digging tools and augers that a group of black clad Elvi were climbing all over, trying to work out the confusing bevy of levers and buttons that controlled the metal monsters. Mr Siddig had left his plant equipment in the hole, awaiting float to the next job. In a effort to distance himself, he had gone on a business trip but had kindly let it slip that the machines would not be moved for 4 weeks and no-one would be on site that entire time. He hoped his trip might provide some some distance and plausible deniability, but figured it would be a stretch, even if he gave them no further assistance. \
\'93Safety is our paramount consideration, you mad Elvises. Let\'92s get some practice before we use these machines in earnest.\'94 Cracker wanted no injuries on his watch. Digging a tunnel under roads and multi-storey buildings was a specialist activity for engineers, but they could not allow a security breach and decided to roll the dice. Each Elvis had brain-dumped every byte of data they could find on the operation excavators and the intricacies of tunnel engineering, but even the most optimistic of them recognised the gulf between theory and practice. They faced death, but were happy to sacrifice themselves for their brothers and sisters. They had a legion of Elvi to use up if their were any injuries or fatalities, each of whom would gladly place themselves in harms way.\
Like a bunch of toddlers in a giant sand pit, they took to the machines and began an infantile and highly dangerous game of dodgem-cars. It took many crashes and some cracked heads for each Elvis to become familiar enough to commence some preliminary excavation. They commenced by using a giant augur, ordinarily used to drill vertical shafts, strapped negligently between two excavators horizontally and they hoed in - balls out. In only a few hours they had dug out a twenty metre shaft, two metres high, before the auger thing collapsed into a spinning mess of metal and dirt.\
\'93The Dawkins save us.\'94 The Great Teacher crossed his fingers and they got to work. To mitigate flooding it was decided to dig the tunnel on a gentle grade upwards for 20 metres before diving under the massive tangle of sewer pipes and sundry conduits blocking a direct path. Despite the keystone-cop start, they made admirable progress until the threat of cave-in became grave and exponentially more likely for each metre of digging. Being unable to secure re-enforcing steel, spray on foam-crete was the best, but most expensive option to sure up the tunnel. The Minister for Finance was furious at the giant chunk of revenue it removed from the coffers, but she was trumped by the Minsters for Defence and Graceland Security. For safety reasons, it would have been preferable to remove the digging equipment before spraying each five metre section, but it took too long and they could not stop to wait for the foam to set. \
The concert above had gotten into full swing, the amplified sound and the approving crowd drowned out any noises that might waft to street level. Most of the digging was now muffled by the length of the tunnel but the concert continued and from all accounts they were raising a fortune. After three days the benefit was wound up, raising more than enough for Mr Siddig to commence reconstruction and with the promised investment from the Elvi, he could secure a loan and the Ulyssian Trade Tower would rise out of the ashes. \
